**Update / Alternative Fix**

I was able to resolve the issue and wanted to share a solution that I consider a bit cleaner than placing the scratch buffer into a dedicated non-cacheable MPU region.

While investigating the DMA write path in the generated `sd_diskio.c`, I noticed the following code:

```

#if (ENABLE_SD_DMA_CACHE_MAINTENANCE == 1)

SCB_InvalidateDCache_by_Addr((uint32_t*)scratch, BLOCKSIZE);

#endif

for (i = 0; i < count; i++)

{

memcpy((void *)scratch, buff, BLOCKSIZE);

buff += BLOCKSIZE;

ret = BSP_SD_WriteBlocks_DMA((uint32_t*)scratch,

(uint32_t)sector++,

1);

}

```

As a first workaround, I moved the `scratch` buffer into a non-cacheable MPU region, which completely eliminated the filesystem corruption.

However, I then tested keeping the scratch buffer in normal cacheable RAM and instead cleaning the cache after the `memcpy()` and before starting the DMA transfer:

```

for (i = 0; i < count; i++)

{

memcpy((void *)scratch, buff, BLOCKSIZE);

#if (ENABLE_SD_DMA_CACHE_MAINTENANCE == 1)

SCB_CleanDCache_by_Addr((uint32_t*)scratch, BLOCKSIZE);

#endif

buff += BLOCKSIZE;

ret = BSP_SD_WriteBlocks_DMA((uint32_t*)scratch,

(uint32_t)sector++,

1);

}

```

This also resolved the issue completely.

With this change, the scratch buffer no longer needs to be placed in a dedicated non-cacheable MPU region and can remain in normal cacheable RAM.

After applying this modification, the SD card remained valid across multiple write cycles, card reinsertions, and power cycles.

Hopefully this helps anyone encountering similar SDMMC DMA / FatFs issues on STM32H7 devices with D-Cache enabled.
